  "local_bulletin": {
    "source_name": "Kenya Meteorological Department (KMD)",
    "text": "Kenya Meteorological Department (KMD) — forecast for Kisumu\nValid for 2026-09-18.\nMax 28.0C / Min 20.0C.\nTonight: Partly cloudy\nMorning: Sunny intervals\nAfternoon: Sunny intervals\nNational outlook mentions this area:\n  - Rainfall is expected in some parts of Kericho, Kakamega, Nandi, Kisumu, Vihiga, Baringo, Bomet,",
    "fetched_at_utc": "2026-09-18T03:03:15.980465Z"
  },
  "yesterday_verification_summary": "At Day+0 verification for yesterday, numerical models correctly captured the dry conditions across the Nyanza Basin with minimal precip errors. Verification results at Day+3 and Day+7 likewise showed strong performance on rainfall calls across all evaluating models.",
  "narrative_markdown": "## Overview\nClearer than yesterday. Dry but thundery. Warming through Monday, with rain becoming more likely.\n\n## Today's Forecast\nDaytime high temperatures near 30°C / 86°F will bring warm conditions across Kisumu, with overnight lows around 18°C / 65°F. Surface winds will be active from the SW, with calibrated peak gusts reaching 35 km/h (19 kt). Solar UV exposure will be very high through midday, reaching a peak index of 9.3. While daylight hours remain predominantly dry, sharp atmospheric instability develops tonight as convective energy climbs, bringing the potential for nocturnal thunderstorms despite sparse overall rainfall amounts. Ground air quality across reporting stations ranges from 40 to 88 (Moderate), with the highest concentration recorded at Kisumu Airport.\n\n## Extended Outlook\nA clear warming trend takes hold from Saturday through Monday, September 21, as daytime highs rise toward 33°C / 91°F. Rain probability increases substantially by Monday, with multi-model guidance showing widespread showers and thunderstorms (80% confidence). From Tuesday through Friday, September 25, conditions fluctuate with lingering mid-week precipitation before drier air attempts to return toward late week.\n\n## Severe Weather / Hazard Potential\nThe primary risk is atmospheric instability tonight through early Saturday morning, with peak convective energy building significantly overnight. Strong, sudden convective gusts remain possible with any thunderstorm overhead or nearby, regardless of low daily rainfall totals or light prevailing surface winds. High solar UV exposure (index 9.3) around midday warrants standard sun protection.\n\n## Winam Gulf — Conditions for Boaters\nOver-lake temperatures will reach a high near 28°C / 82°F. Surface wind gusts across the water are expected to peak near 29 km/h (16 kt). Significant convective energy is forecast to build over the gulf after dark, creating a risk of sudden thunder and localized squalls overnight. Boaters should exercise caution during night operations on the water.\n\n## Detailed Discussion\n### Synoptic Overview\nAcross roughly 2,600 km, pressure is lowest toward the northeast (1008 hPa) and highest toward the southeast (1018 hPa) — a 10 hPa spread, a strong large-scale gradient. Pressure overhead is near-steady. Across the Nyanza Basin, mean surface pressure sits near 1013.6 hPa with a gentle 24-hour tendency of -0.3 hPa. Regional MSLP spans from 1013.6 hPa near Kisumu to 1015.8 hPa across the southern highlands, maintaining mild lake-breeze convergence during the day and land-breeze drainage tonight.\n\n### Forecaster Confidence Notes\nGuidance weighting today relies heavily on Best Match for rainfall calls at Day+0, given its established record as the top rain caller (84% historical accuracy). Uncorrected model gusts from ECMWF and ICON under-forecast peak surface winds, so peak gusts were adjusted upward to the calibrated value of 35.1 km/h. Convective energy displays notable model divergence tonight: GFS remains suppressed near 200 J/kg, while UKMO surges past 2600 J/kg and ECMWF reaches 1850 J/kg; the thundery wording reflects this elevated upper-bound potential.\n\nThe Kenya Meteorological Department (KMD) bulletin forecasts a high of 28.0°C / 82°F and low of 20.0°C / 68°F for Kisumu today, with sunny intervals by day and partly cloudy conditions tonight. This aligns with the numerical consensus for dry daytime conditions, though KMD leans slightly cooler on daytime highs than GFS or Best Match.\n\nGround air quality station breakdown: Kisumu Airport reports AQI 88 (PM2.5: 88.0 µg/m³), Ochieng' Avenue reports AQI 40 (PM2.5: 40.0 µg/m³), and Dunga Beach reports AQI 64 (PM2.5: 64.0 µg/m³). The guidance behind this forecast was last aligned at 18:00Z (9.0 hours ago).",
